  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/ba/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_117.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A radio report goes out, announcing that the Eel has gotten out of prison on good behavior.&nbsp; Johnny mistakenly believes he's <i>escaped</i> from prison and confronts him, but the warden assures him he's out legitimately, and Eel threatens to sue for the treatment!&nbsp; Leopold Stryke (Eel) even gets his old job back as caretaker of an ocean view aquarium, but we soon learn he <i>hasn't </i>changed his tune.&nbsp; Happy that his \"specially-built helicopter\" is still at his disposal, and armed with some new upgrades to his suit, he prepares to strike out again as the Eel!\n\nKnowing the Torch will be monitoring banks and jewelry stores, looking for signs of the Eel, he instead goes after a valuable stamp collection.&nbsp; Johnny sees the announcement in the paper the next day, but there's no proof it was the Eel.&nbsp; Meanwhile, at the aquarium, Stryke scours the paper and notes that a valuable ruby from Turkistan is in NYC!&nbsp; That night he sneaks into the hotel to grab the ruby but instead finds the Human Torch waiting for him!&nbsp; But the Eel escapes in his helicopter after spraying Johnny with laughing gas.\n\nSue helps Johnny realize that the pontoons on the bottom of the copter mean it's water-based, causing Johnny to hunt for the Eel back at the aquarium.&nbsp; But Stryke sees him examining the exhibits and becomes the Eel, grabbing Johnny and holding him tightly thanks to the asbestos grease he's coated his costume with.&nbsp; He briefly subdues the Torch, but Johnny recovers before he can be thrown into a shark tank.\n\nHe melts the tank containing the asbestos grease, and the Eel slips on it, plunging down into the tank filled with electric eels, and Johnny helps him out... right into the hands of the waiting police.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A villain nobody needed returns for a story nobody asked for.\nBy the final page, the biggest mystery is how any of this seemed like a good idea.",
  "subtitle": "The Eel Gets Another Chance.&nbsp; WHY?",
  "review_text": "The Eel had first appeared only five months earlier, in <i>Strange Tales</i> 112 ([LINK:1963:ST_112_torch0|ST #112|2026-11-04]), and yet... <i>somehow</i>... he's already out on good behavior.\n\n[ASSET: right | FalseArrest | width=13%] Johnny's blunder at the beginning of the story is quite understandable, for <i>who in their right mind</i> would think that a guy who was identified as the \"Living Bomb\" during his one previous appearance could possibly have served his sentence within that short a time?\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(The old \"short prison sentence\" trope is one of the hallmarks of Silver Age storytelling.)]\n\n[ASSET: left | Whirlybird | width=15%] On page 7, the Eel jumps off of a penthouse roof and plummets downward, forcing the Human Torch to go after him, thinking that he's doing him a favor by saving his life, but by the end of the page, we learn that the Eel jumped <i>down</i> into his waiting helicopter.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]\n[BR]\n[BR] Exactly <i>how</i> does one jump [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=downward] to the safety of a hovering helicopter without getting completely [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=shredded] by its propeller blades?\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=This sort of plotting absolutely <i>smacks</i> of lackadaisical, placeholder storytelling!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAnd even worse was the page 8 suggestion that the Human Torch does not know how to find his opponent, claiming that [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"he won't be in the <i>phone book</i>!\"]&nbsp; This is unbelievable, because if the man actually <i>did</i> serve a prison sentence, his true identity would have been revealed during his trial, and it would have been an easy matter for Johnny to learn that he was once again employed by the aquarium.\n[ASSET: center | NotInPhoneBook | width=67%]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=But <i>no!</i>]&nbsp; It takes help from his sister Sue before Johnny realizes that... [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold-italic; text=DUH!] -- maybe he ought to check where the Eel used to work.\n\nIs it any <i>wonder</i> why Johnny isn't a solo crime fighter?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Holy cow!</i>)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe Torch eventually tricks the Eel into falling into his own tank filled with electric eels, and readers are then subjected to the standard ending of [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=\"I'll get you <i>yet</i>!\"]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Spare me...)]\n[ASSET: center | IntoHisOwnTank | width=67%]\n\nThe speed at which the ending comes -- and the tidiness of it all -- is [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=nauseating], making the entire story feel like a waste of 12 cents!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis was about the lamest follow-up story to what was originally an \"unimaginative\" villain in the first place (the very word I used in my original review of the character).\n\nThe Eel's threat of <i>suing</i> the Torch for \"assault and battery\" at the beginning of the story is unceremoniously dropped, as if it never happened, which is a shame, [SPAN: severity=info; text=because the Torch having to defend himself would have made for better reading than <i>this</i>!]\n\nFurthermore, the other members of the FF keep showing up every few pages, only to encourage Johnny to keep at it and not to give up, [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=but they obviously can't be bothered to help in any way!]\n\nThe story provided <b>no enjoyment whatsoever</b>, and I can easily envision readers of the day <i>racing</i> for their stationery to beg Stan [SPAN: severity=warning; text=<b>never</b> to bring back the Eel again!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(He was bad enough the <i>first</i> time!)]\n\nThe plot of this story (such as it is) can't have taken more than five minutes to come up with and seems to check just about every box in [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=\"Return of the <i>fill-in-the-blank</i>\"] storytelling and the trope-filled toolkit that goes with it.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=\"Return of the Heartburn!\"] would have been more appropriate!\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; text=What a waste of time!]",
  "highlight": "The Human Torch takes on the Eel's return engagement, while readers wonder who asked for one."
}